gambatte (libretro/gambatte-libretro 77f2567): its network Game Link as two
handhelds on Wi-Fi need it (ZyperOS's Link Cable, src/link.h).

- A peer that went away without closing (out of range, its battery out)
  left the side waiting for its answer blocked in read() until TCP gave up,
  the game frozen for minutes. Each read and write now waits at most ten
  seconds (SO_RCVTIMEO, SO_SNDTIMEO), and a timeout is the cable pulled:
  0xFF to the game, and the connection made again.
- A write to a peer that had reset the connection raised SIGPIPE, which
  ends the emulator: send() with MSG_NOSIGNAL (SO_NOSIGPIPE where there is
  none).
- The two bytes of a transfer are read and written whole; a second segment
  was read as garbage.
- check() read a peer that had closed as "nothing yet" for ever, so that
  side never connected again: a closed peer now closes the socket.
- A lost connection is tried again every second on a monotonic clock. It
  was five seconds of clock(), the process's CPU time: up to half a minute
  on a handheld.
- A connect to an address nobody answers waits 500 ms, not the seconds of
  an ARP timeout on the game's thread.
- TCP_NODELAY, and SO_REUSEADDR on the server, so a second game hosting
  right after the first is not refused its port (TIME_WAIT).
- A byte read in the middle of a transfer took the value's top bits again
  at every read, so a game that waits on SC read every byte as 0x00 or
  0xFF (its top bit eight times). The bits now come in in order; found with
  tools/gblink-loopback.c, two cores linked on a computer.
- retro_unload_game ends the link. A frontend that keeps the core loaded
  between games (Diatom) kept the socket open after the game, and the other
  handheld waited on it.

